// Package match ranks library titles against what Alexa heard and decides whether to play the best one, ask which one was meant, or report nothing found.
//
// An Index holds one library in one language and runs this pipeline per Search:
//
//  1. Normalise. Normalize folds case and diacritics (keeping the combining marks that spell Devanagari vowels and kana voicing, and dropping Arabic and Hebrew vowel points), writes every script's digits as ASCII, drops punctuation and leading articles, joins runs of single letters ("E.T." -> "et"), turns roman numerals after a word, number words, ordinals (spelt out or written with digits) and spoken years into digits ("rocky second" -> "rocky 2", "6th" -> "6", "nineteen eighty four" -> "1984"), splitting German and Italian numbers written as one word into their number words first ("millenovecentonovantadue" -> "1992"), reads "½" as the language says "and a half" and drops "part"/"chapter" before a number. Each supported language (en, de, fr, es, it) has its own articles, number words and conjunctions; others use English rules. Titles and queries go through the same function, so both sides meet in the middle.
//  2. Alias. At Upsert every Doc expands to aliases: its title and Doc.Aliases, a variant with single-letter roman numerals converted ("Rocky V" -> "rocky 5"), German umlauts spelt out ("ü" -> "ue"), and derived shortenings that drop a subtitle, keep only the subtitle or drop a parenthetical ("Dune: Part Two" -> "Dune", "Avengers: Endgame" -> "Endgame"). Derived aliases score with a penalty and never count as exact.
//  3. Retrieve. An in-memory inverted index finds a candidate pool of about 800 Docs with a disjunction of fuzzy word matches (within one or two edits of a word, keeping its first letter), phonetic key matches (from internal/fuzzy/phonetic: Kölner Phonetik for German, keying a final "-er" as the "-a" it is spoken as, French and Spanish encoders for those languages, Double Metaphone for the rest) and, when those find little, character trigrams, ranked by TF-IDF. Docs whose primary alias equals the query are always added. Retrieval only needs recall; its scores are never exposed. A simpler trigram retriever can stand in for small libraries.
//  4. Score. Each candidate is scored against each alias and keeps its best: s = max(token_set_ratio, 0.9 * partial_ratio when the query is shorter, ratio without spaces, 0.9 when the strings sound identical), then 0.45*s + 0.25*Jaro-Winkler + 0.15*OSA similarity + 0.15*phonetic overlap, scaled down for query words the alias does not account for (numbers must match exactly). An exact match of a primary alias scores 1 and fuzzy matches are capped below it. A trailing release year in the query ("dune 2021") is matched against Doc.Year. Doc.Boost and Query.Hints add at most Config.MaxBoost.
//  5. Decide. With c the top score and m its lead over the runner-up: play when exactly one Doc matched exactly, when c >= 0.90 (with a lead of at least 0.02), or when c >= 0.80 and m >= 0.10; in French, whose keys merge silent endings, a top candidate that only sounds like the query ("son" for "sont") always needs m >= 0.10; a top candidate whose best alias lacks a number the query carries ("crocodile dundee 2" against "Crocodile Dundee") is a missing sequel and asks instead of playing; ask when several Docs matched exactly or c >= 0.60; otherwise not found. Thresholds live in Config.
package match
